FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1301 — the only test covering the new `Test` frame does not actually test the claim in its own message. The final assertion checks `goto_definition(source, 9, 5)` on `item`, which is a `for each` variable whose `For` scope is already popped at line 5's `end` (src/lsp.rs:835-841 pops only `opens_scope`/`catch_scope` frames). It would pass even if a declaration written directly in the `test` body leaked. `src/lsp.rs:963` pushes `Frame::block(FrameKind::Test)` with `opens_scope: false`, so a plain `set local to 1` in a test body is declared in the enclosing scope and survives the test's `end`. If `test` bodies are meant to be scoped (the analyzer pushes a scope for `Test` at src/analyzer.rs:471/488), navigation is wrong here. Add a fixture with a direct `set local` in the test body and assert it is `None` after `end`; if the runtime does isolate test bodies, give the `Test` frame `Frame::scoped`. VERIFIED: read src/lsp.rs:835-841, src/lsp.rs:963 and tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1262-1306; the assertion at 1301-1305 cannot observe a Test-frame leak.

2. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:515 — `declare_fresh` inserts with `scope.entry(name).or_insert(id)`, so plain-name resolution keeps the first binding while `object_fields`/`object_methods` keep the last. Combined with the plain lookup at src/lsp.rs:1271-1274, a bare call to a method that shares its spelling with an earlier-declared field resolves to the field, even though `member_binding` (src/lsp.rs:739-748) resolves the same spelling to the method when called through a receiver. E.g. `object R / has b default 1 / to can b() ... end / to can a() give back b() end` resolves `b()` to the field. Concrete fix: when the identifier is followed by `LeftParen`, prefer a `DefinitionKind::Function` binding from the visible scope (mirroring `member_binding`'s call rule). VERIFIED: traced src/lsp.rs:499-518, 739-748 and 1271-1274.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
